  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mariamma Kurian vs The Food Inspector on 3 February, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Wednesday, The 3Rd Day Of February 2016/14Th Magha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 2663 of 2005 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 382/2002 of Addl.Sessions (Adhoc-1) Court, Kottayam Cc 245/1998 of J.M.F.C.,Erattupetta Revision Petitioner(S)/Appellant/2Nd Accused,: ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- Mariamma Kurian, Muthu Punnackal House, Erattupetta. By Adv. Sri.M.P.Madhavankutty Respondent(S)/Complainant And State.: --------------------------------------------------------------- 1. The Food Inspector, Erattupetta Grama Panchayath, Erattupetta. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. R1 & R2 By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 03-02-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.2663 of 2005 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Mariamma Kurian vs The Food Inspector on 3 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/50325753/ 1 Dated this the 3rd day of February 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The second accused in C.C.No.245 of 1998 on the files of the Court of the Judicial Magistrate of First Class, Erattupetta filed this Revision Petition challenging the concurrent finding of conviction and sentence passed by the courts below under Sections 16(1)(a)(ii) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 read with Rule 50(1) of Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 17.3.1998 at about 2 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector, purchased three packets of banana chips, each packet having a weight of 200 gram each, from the shop of the first accused. The second accused was the licensee of the said shop. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P13 report stating that the sample contained synthetic food colour tartarzine and therefore, the same was adulterated under the P.F.A. Rules. The revision petitioner did not have the required license under the P.F.A. Rules. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts. P1 to P26 and X1 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 and DW2 were examined and Exts.D1 to D3 were marked for the revision petitioner. However, the revision petitioner was convicted for the offence under Sections 16(1)(a)(ii) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 read with Rule 50(1) of Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955, for violation of the provisions of license. The courts below concurrently convicted the revision petitioner under Section 16(1)(a)(ii) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 read with Rule 50(1) of Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955.",
